Here's an honest breakdown of my process and how it addresses each of your questions:Quality of chosen experts:
I didn't just take the first Google results. My process was search using keyword combinations like "LinkedIn marketing" and "B2B SaaS"  to surface real
people then manually verify each person's actual profile, their real posts, dates, and background before including them. I cross-checked details like 
follower counts and whether their content was genuinely hands-on before adding anyone to the list. A few names I found through general search didn't 
hold up on closer inspection (mixed unrelated content, unclear credentials) and I excluded them rather than adding to the list.

Repository structure:
Organized as planned — research/sources.md for the master list with annotations, research/linkedin-posts/ with one file per expert containing their 
posts, dates, and links. I also used Link shorten tools instead of raw long URLs to keep everything clean and readable on GitHub, since I didn't have 
access to a URL-shortening API — I did this manually, formatting each link by hand.Ability to work with APIs and technical tools:

Important limitation to flag : 
LinkedIn does not offer a official public API for scraping post content, and it actively blocks automated/bot
access to profile and post pages. This meant I couldn't collect LinkedIn data programmatically the way the original instructions suggested 
for YouTube. Instead, I used a manual process: searching for each expert, reading their public posts directly, and using AI to help me 
research, verify, and write up structured summaries of their background and content in my own words. For YouTube, most of the experts I selected 
are LinkedIn-native creators without an active YouTube channel, so that data source wasn't applicable for most of them — a couple of exceptions 
had channels but their primary content platform was clearly LinkedIn
